[enso-index] diagnostic figure written to /home/clairev/Research/FNO-koopman/neural_koopman/figures/enso/enso_index_prediction_skill.pdf

Figure caption:
Niño-3.4 forecast skill on ERSSTv5, leads 1–12. The BSO forecasts the index directly: a reduced state — a training-fit 12-dimensional PCA of the 12-month delay embedding of 20 weighted EOF coefficients — is advanced by a frozen neural operator, a dictionary Ψ(z) = [1, z, h(z)] is fitted against that operator by the Galerkin invariance objective, and per-lead coefficients are regressed on the dictionary. LIM is the classical benchmark on the same 20 EOF coefficients, fitted by ordinary least squares: its regularisation is the PC truncation, so no ridge is applied to it. Every method is fitted on the same origins and verified on the same launches. A per-lead linear regression on the same state (not shown) is separated from the BSO only at leads 2–5, 7–8 months by a 95 % block bootstrap on the paired difference; the full comparison, including that control, the propagated-operator rollout and a matched-input network, is reported in the accompanying table. Climatology has undefined anomaly correlation (a zero-anomaly forecast has zero variance) so it appears in the error panel alone, drawn as its lead-average. Damped persistence has exactly the correlation of plain persistence, because correlation is invariant to the positive per-lead scaling γ^τ; damping is visible in RMSE only.
